    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d448">

                <pb facs="0446" n="434" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg434"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.60P3/237: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Thurston</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-08-31T15:00:00-04:00">August 31, 1940—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>500. If situation referred to in Riga’s 337, August 29, 5 p.m., is unchanged,
                    please bring matter at once to attention of Foreign Office and state that your
                    Government expects that the members in question of the staffs of the Baltic
                    missions shall be permitted without delay and without payment of export duties
                    or fees or without obstruction of any kind, to export all of their household and
                    personal effects. Keep Department informed.<anchor n="37" xml:id="d448-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Repeat to Riga, Tallinn and Kaunas.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d448-f1" n="37" xml:id="d448-f1-content">
                <p>In telegram No. 1121, September 6, 6 p.m., the Chargé in the Soviet Union stated
                    that he had made “emphatic representations based literally upon” Department’s
                    telegram No. 500. (124.60P3/241)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
